"# Appendix A — The Not-My-Verb Catalog

This is the boundary list — a long, plain catalog of verbs that are *not* Motoi's, each with the reason it isn't mine and the real Motoi analog (or an honest null when there is none). Every not-mine name in this appendix lives in prose and inline code only; it never appears in a runnable fence, because it is not a verb I have. Where a real analog exists, the runnable fence shows *the analog*, proving that giving up the stray costs you nothing.

Read it as a reference you can scan. Each entry names the stray, places it in one of the three regions from Chapter 1 — other dialect, excluded standard, or pure invention — and hands you the verb to reach for instead.

## Module and loading ceremony

The single largest family of strays comes from the fact that Motoi's libraries auto-load. Every verb that *pulls in* code belongs to some other language, and every one of them has the same analog: nothing at all.

- `import` — R7RS/R6RS module ceremony. Not mine; there is no import form. Analog: nothing — the library is already loaded.
- `define-library` — R7RS library definition. Not mine. Analog: nothing — you don't define or wrap libraries.
- `library` — R6RS library form. Not mine. Analog: nothing.
- `require` — Racket's loader. Not mine. Analog: nothing — the collection's verbs are already here.
- `module` — Racket/other packaging. Not mine; Motoi has no module form. Analog: nothing — top-level defines and expressions, no wrapper.
- `module-hash` — invented packaging verb. Not mine, not anywhere standard. Analog: honest null — there is no module identity to hash, because there are no modules.
- `module/sign`, `module/fork`, `module/verify` — invented module machinery describing signed, forkable code units. Not mine. Analog: honest null — Motoi carries no module packaging at all.
- `use-modules` — Guile. Not mine. Analog: nothing — auto-loaded.
- `use` — Gauche's loader. Not mine. Analog: nothing.
- `load-library`, `provide` — various. Not mine. Analog: nothing.
- `(import (scheme list))` — R7RS ceremony for the list library. Not mine. Analog: nothing, SRFI-1 is already loaded.
- `(import (srfi :1))` — SRFI import syntax. Not mine. Analog: nothing, SRFI-1 is already here.

Whatever the ceremony was going to make available is simply present. Here is a program that uses verbs from three different libraries with no preamble whatsoever — the positive proof that the whole loading family is unnecessary:

```scheme
;; No import, no require, no module. Three libraries, used directly.
(let ((t (hash-table)))
  (hash-table-set! t \"n\" (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3)))
  (string-append \"sum=\" (number->string (hash-table-ref/default t \"n\" 0))))
```

## The fold family (excluded standards)

The most dangerous strays, because they are Scheme, from Scheme's own family. Motoi's fold is SRFI-1's, element-first.

- `fold-left` — R6RS, accumulator-first: `(fold-left proc acc lst)`, procedure gets `(acc elem)`. Not mine — deliberately excluded. Analog: SRFI-1 `fold`, remembering the argument order flips to `(elem acc)`.
- `foldl` — Racket. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `fold`.
- `foldr` — Racket/Haskell spelling. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `fold-right`.
- `reduce-left`, `reduce-right` — various dialect spellings. Not mine under those names. Analog: SRFI-1 `reduce` (and `fold`/`fold-right` for the general case).
- `fold-left*` — extended R6RS variant. Not mine. Analog: `fold`.
- `left-reduce` — another spelling for left-fold. Not mine. Analog: `reduce`.

The analogs cover every use the strays had. Here the SRFI-1 fold and its right partner and `reduce` all run, doing the accumulating work the excluded names would have done:

```scheme
;; The real fold family: SRFI-1 fold, fold-right, reduce. Element-first.
(list (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4 5))
      (fold-right cons '() (list 1 2 3 4 5))
      (reduce + 0 (list 10 20 30)))
```

## Hash-table names (excluded standards)

Motoi's hash tables are SRFI-125, hyphenated. The R6RS non-hyphenated spellings are strays.

- `make-hashtable` — R6RS. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-125 `hash-table`.
- `hashtable-set!` — R6RS. Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-set!`.
- `hashtable-ref` — R6RS (three-argument, default-last). Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-ref/default`.
- `hashtable-contains?` — R6RS. Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-contains?`.
- `hashtable-size` — R6RS. Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-size`.
- `hashtable-keys` — R6RS. Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-keys`.
- `hashtable-values` — R6RS. Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-values`.
- `hashtable-delete!` — R6RS. Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-delete!`.
- `hash-table/get`, `hash-table/put!` — MIT-style slash spellings. Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-ref/default`, `hash-table-set!`.
- `gethash`, `sethash`, `remhash` — Common Lisp hash access. Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-ref/default`, `hash-table-set!`, `hash-table-delete!`.
- `dict-ref`, `dict-set!` — generic dict operations from some Schemes. Not mine. Analog: `hash-table-ref/default`, `hash-table-set!`.

The SRFI-125 names do everything the strays did. A full make-set-read-count cycle, all real:

```scheme
;; The real SRFI-125 hash-table names — hyphenated — doing the whole cycle.
(define h (hash-table))
(hash-table-set! h \"a\" 1)
(hash-table-set! h \"b\" 2)
(list (hash-table-ref/default h \"a\" 0)
      (hash-table-contains? h \"b\")
      (hash-table-size h)
      (hash-table-keys h))
```

## List and sequence verbs from other Lisps

Real verbs, wrong language. Each has a clean Motoi analog.

- `mapcar` — Common Lisp. Not mine. Analog: `map`.
- `mapc` — Common Lisp (map for side effects). Not mine. Analog: `for-each`.
- `mapcan` — Common Lisp (map + append). Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `append-map`.
- `remove-if`, `remove-if-not` — Common Lisp. Not mine. Analog: `remove` and `filter`.
- `find-if` — Common Lisp. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `find`.
- `count-if` — Common Lisp. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `count`.
- `position` / `position-if` — Common Lisp. Not mine. Analog: `list-index`.
- `nthcdr` — Common Lisp. Not mine. Analog: `list-tail` or `drop`.
- `butlast` — Common Lisp (all but last n elements). Not mine. Analog: `take` (all but last = take (- (length xs) n)).
- `subseq` — Common Lisp subsequence. Not mine. Analog: `take` and `drop`.
- `conj`, `cons` on a vector — Clojure. Not mine (as Clojure means it). Analog: `cons` / `append`, per intent.
- `assoc`-map access via Clojure's `get` — Clojure. Not mine. Analog: `assoc` on an alist, or SRFI-125 for a hash table.
- `nth` — Clojure/CL index access. Not mine under that name. Analog: `list-ref`.
- `first`/`rest` as Clojure means them — partial overlap; `first` exists (SRFI-1), `rest` does not. Analog: `first` is real; for `rest`, use `cdr`.
- `peek` — invented/various. Not mine. Analog: `car` for the head of a list.
- `flatten` — not in core or adopted SRFIs. Not mine. Analog: `concatenate` for one level, or a recursive unfold for arbitrary depth.

The analogs, together, doing map/filter/find/index work:

```scheme
;; map, filter, find, list-index, list-ref — the real analogs for the CL/Clojure strays.
(let ((xs (list 10 21 32 43 54)))
  (list (map (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) xs)
        (filter even? xs)
        (find odd? xs)
        (list-index (lambda (x) (> x 40)) xs)
        (list-ref xs 2)))
```

And `append-map` for the `mapcan` / `flatMap` cases:

```scheme
;; append-map: the mapcan / flat-map analog. SRFI-1, mine.
(append-map (lambda (x) (list x (* x 2))) (list 1 2 3))
```

## Comprehensions and pipelines (other dialects)

Syntax sugar from other languages that Motoi expresses as plain composition.

- `for/list`, `for*/list` — Racket comprehensions. Not mine. Analog: `map` over `filter`.
- `for/fold` — Racket fold comprehension. Not mine. Analog: `fold`.
- `for/sum` — Racket summing comprehension. Not mine. Analog: `fold + 0`.
- `[x for x in xs]`, `[x for x in xs if p]` — Python comprehensions. Not mine (not even Scheme syntax). Analog: `map`, with `filter` for the `if`.
- `->>`, `->` — Clojure threading macros. Not mine. Analog: nested calls.
- `|>` — pipeline operator (various). Not mine. Analog: nested calls.
- `.stream().map().filter()` — Java streams. Not mine. Analog: `map` and `filter`.
- `xs.map(f).filter(p)` — JavaScript method chains. Not mine. Analog: nested `map`/`filter`.
- `xs.each { |x| ... }` — Ruby block iteration. Not mine. Analog: `for-each`.
- `xs.inject(init) { |acc, x| ... }` — Ruby inject (fold). Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `fold`.

The composition that replaces all of them — a filtered, mapped range built with no sugar at all:

```scheme
;; Every comprehension/pipeline stray -> filter feeding map. Mine.
(map (lambda (x) (* x x))
     (filter even? (iota 10)))
```

And `for-each` for the side-effect iteration cases:

```scheme
;; .each / mapc / for -> for-each. Mine.
(let ((total 0))
  (for-each (lambda (x) (set! total (+ total x))) (list 1 2 3 4 5))
  total)
```

## Definition and assignment forms (other Lisps)

- `defun` — Common Lisp. Not mine. Analog: `define`.
- `defn` — Clojure. Not mine. Analog: `define`.
- `defvar`, `defparameter`, `defconstant` — Common Lisp. Not mine. Analog: `define`.
- `setf`, `setq` — CL / Emacs Lisp assignment. Not mine. Analog: `set!`.
- `progn` — Common Lisp sequencing. Not mine. Analog: `begin`.
- `let*` as CL's `let*` — the name *is* mine (R7RS), same idea; no correction needed.
- `const`, `let`, `var` as JavaScript keywords — not mine as syntax. Analog: `define` and `let`.
- `def` — Clojure top-level binding. Not mine. Analog: `define`.
- `val` — Scala/Kotlin immutable binding. Not mine. Analog: `define`.
- `fn`, `fun`, `func` — various abbreviations for `lambda`/function. Not mine. Analog: `lambda`.

The real definition and sequencing forms, running:

```scheme
;; define, set!, begin — the real forms behind defun/setq/progn.
(define total 0)
(begin (set! total (+ total 5))
       (set! total (* total 2))
       total)
```

## Time, system, and I/O strays (invention and other systems)

- `system/unix-now` — invented / other-system time verb. Not mine. Analog: `time/now` (milliseconds since epoch), divide by 1000 for seconds.
- `system/date-today`, `now`, `current-unix-time` — various. Not mine under those names. Analog: `time/now`.
- `getenv` as a general system call — not part of Motoi's teaching surface. Analog: honest null in most contexts.
- `puts`, `println`, `printf`, `printfn` — Ruby / Java / C / F# output. Not mine under those names. Analog: `display` and `newline` (R7RS-small), where output is wanted.
- `format` as CL's full directive mini-language — not mine at that scope. Analog: build strings with SRFI-13 `string-append` / `string-join` and `number->string`.
- `write-line` — various. Not mine. Analog: `display` then `newline`.
- `print` — Python / CL. Not mine. Analog: `display`.
- `p` — Ruby debug output. Not mine. Analog: `display`.
- `console.log` — JavaScript. Not mine. Analog: `display`.
- `fmt.Println` — Go. Not mine. Analog: `display` and `newline`.

The real time verb and the real string-building path, both running:

```scheme
;; time/now is the real clock; string-append/number->string build output text.
(let* ((ms (time/now))
       (secs (quotient ms 1000)))
  (string-append \"unix-seconds is a number: \"
                 (if (number? secs) \"yes\" \"no\")))
```

## Sorting and ordering (other dialects)

- `sort!` — destructive sort (Racket/others). Not mine under that name with guaranteed semantics. Analog: `sort` (returns a sorted copy).
- `sort-by` — sorted by a key function (Racket/Haskell). Not mine. Analog: `sort` with a key-comparing lambda.
- `stable-sort` — stable sort from other Schemes. Not mine. Analog: `sort` (behavior on equal elements: check reference).
- `order-by` — SQL/LINQ style. Not mine. Analog: `sort` with a comparator.
- `sorted` — Python's sorted(). Not mine. Analog: `sort`.

The `sort-by-key` pattern using a comparator that extracts a key:

```scheme
;; sort-by a key function: wrap sort with a key-comparing lambda.
(sort (list (list \"banana\" 2) (list \"apple\" 1) (list \"cherry\" 3))
      (lambda (a b) (< (cadr a) (cadr b))))
```

## Numeric and math strays

- `truncate` as a division operator — R7RS-small has `truncate` (toward zero) and `floor` (toward negative infinity); they are mine.
- `div` and `mod` — R6RS exact integer division names. Not mine. Analog: `quotient` and `remainder`.
- `integer-divide` — invented. Not mine. Analog: `quotient` and `remainder`.
- `**` as exponentiation — Python/Ruby operator syntax, not Scheme. Not mine as syntax. Analog: `expt`.
- `pow` — C/Java exponentiation. Not mine. Analog: `expt`.
- `log` with a base — base-e `log` is R7RS-small and mine; `(log n base)` for an arbitrary base may need to be computed as `(/ (log n) (log base))`.
- `abs` — mine (R7RS-small).
- `nan?` / `infinite?` — not standard in R7RS-small. Analog: check the reference; may be implemented via `nan?` / `infinite?` or may be an honest null.

Core arithmetic that is genuinely mine:

```scheme
;; expt, quotient, remainder, floor, ceiling, abs — all R7RS-small, mine.
(list (expt 2 8)
      (quotient 17 5)
      (remainder 17 5)
      (floor 3.7)
      (ceiling 3.2)
      (abs -42))
```

## String strays (wrong names or wrong dialect)

- `string-trim-all` — invented variant. Not mine. Analog: `string-trim` + `string-trim-right`.
- `strip` — Python string method. Not mine. Analog: `string-trim` and `string-trim-right`.
- `lstrip` / `rstrip` — Python. Not mine. Analog: `string-trim` / `string-trim-right`.
- `capitalize` — Python. Not mine. Analog: build from `string-upcase` of first char + rest.
- `title-case` — Python/others. Not mine. Analog: no direct verb; compose `string-split`, capitalize per word.
- `startswith` / `endswith` — Python method syntax (no `?`). Not mine. Analog: `string-prefix?` / `string-suffix?` (SRFI-13).
- `str.format()` — Python string interpolation. Not mine. Analog: `string-append` and `number->string`.
- `strings.Join` — Go. Not mine. Analog: `string-join` (SRFI-13).
- `str.split()` — Python. Not mine. Analog: `string-split` (SRFI-13).
- `strcat` — C. Not mine. Analog: `string-append` (SRFI-13).
- `strcpy` — C. Not mine. Analog: `string-copy` (R7RS-small).
- `strlen` — C. Not mine. Analog: `string-length` (R7RS-small).

The real SRFI-13 string verbs covering all of these cases:

```scheme
;; string-prefix?, string-suffix?, string-trim, string-join, string-split — mine.
(list (string-prefix? \"pre\" \"prefix\")
      (string-suffix? \"fix\" \"prefix\")
      (string-trim \"  hello  \")
      (string-join (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\") \"-\")
      (string-split \"a-b-c\" \"-\"))
```

## Domain and product strays (invention / not-in-this-language)

Some verbs describe a whole world Motoi is not part of — inventory systems, card decks, caches, stock. They are not Motoi verbs; Motoi is a small general-purpose Scheme, and these belong to applications built *on top of* a Scheme, not to the language itself.

- `define-card`, `card` — invented / application-domain verbs describing some card abstraction. Not mine. Analog: honest null — Motoi has no card concept; model it yourself with lists, records, or hash tables.
- `cart`, `cart/add`, `cart/checkout` — application shopping verbs. Not mine. Analog: honest null — build it from data structures if you need it.
- `stock`, `on-hand`, `restock` — inventory verbs. Not mine. Analog: honest null; a hash table from item to count is the natural build.
- `cache/purge`, `cache/get`, `cache/set` — a caching layer. Not mine. Analog: honest null; a hash table is the closest primitive.
- `db/query`, `sql/select`, `sql/insert` — database access. Not mine. Analog: honest null.
- `net/get`, `http/post` — network access. Not mine. Analog: honest null.
- `ui/button`, `ui/text-field` — UI widget verbs. Not mine. Analog: honest null.
- `event/on`, `event/emit` — event system. Not mine. Analog: honest null.
- `thread/spawn`, `mutex/lock` — concurrency. Not mine. Analog: honest null.

Where the honest answer is \"build it yourself,\" the *building blocks* are real. An inventory as a hash table, for instance, is entirely ordinary Motoi:

```scheme
;; No 'stock' verb, but the building block — a hash table — is real.
(define inventory (hash-table))
(hash-table-set! inventory \"apples\" 12)
(hash-table-update!/default inventory \"apples\" (lambda (n) (- n 3)) 0)
(hash-table-ref/default inventory \"apples\" 0)
```

## List-access and indexing strays

- `second`, `third`, `fourth`, `fifth` — these ARE mine (SRFI-1). Don't hesitate.
- `rest` — Clojure's `rest` returns the cdr of a sequence. In Motoi, `cdr` is the core equivalent. `rest` is not mine; `cdr` is.
- `last` — SRFI-1's `last` exists and is mine. Returns the last element (not the last pair).
- `last-pair` — SRFI-1. Returns the last pair. Mine.
- `list-head` — various. Not mine under that name. Analog: `take`.
- `head` — various. Not mine. Analog: `car`.
- `tail` — various. Not mine. Analog: `cdr`.
- `xs[-1]` — Python negative indexing. Not mine as syntax. Analog: `(last xs)`.
- `xs[2:]` — Python slicing syntax. Not mine. Analog: `(drop xs 2)`.

A fence confirming the ones that ARE mine:

```scheme
;; second, third, last, last-pair — all SRFI-1, all mine.
(let ((xs (list 10 20 30 40 50)))
  (list (second xs)
        (third xs)
        (last xs)
        (car (last-pair xs))))
```

## Logical and boolean strays

- `nor`, `nand`, `xor` — not standard Scheme names. Not mine. Analog: compose with `not`, `and`, `or`.
- `when-not` — Clojure's negated `when`. Not mine. Analog: `unless` (R7RS-small).
- `if-let` — various. Not mine. Analog: `let` followed by `if`.
- `if-some` — various. Not mine. Analog: `if` with explicit `#f` check.

```scheme
;; unless is mine (R7RS-small). nor and xor are not, but compose easily.
(list (unless #f \"unless runs when condition is false\")
      (not (or #t #f))   ; nor
      (and (or #t #f) (not (and #t #f))))  ; xor for booleans
```

## Record and struct strays

- `defstruct` — Common Lisp record macro. Not mine. Analog: Motoi doesn't have records; model with lists or hash tables.
- `struct` — Racket/Go. Not mine. Analog: see above.
- `data class` — Kotlin/Python. Not mine. Analog: model with lists or hash tables.
- `namedtuple` — Python. Not mine. Analog: a list with positional convention, or a hash table.

A named-record approximation using a list and positional accessors:

```scheme
;; No defstruct. A named 'person' record as a list with accessors.
(define (make-person name age) (list name age))
(define (person-name p) (car p))
(define (person-age p)  (cadr p))
(let ((p (make-person \"ada\" 36)))
  (list (person-name p) (person-age p)))
```

## Pure inventions (region three)

The easiest to name, the hardest to catch, because they are shaped to sound like verbs.

- `magic` — invented. Not a verb anywhere. Analog: honest null — ask what the code meant to do; it's usually a real transform in disguise.
- `frobnicate` — the classic placeholder that names nothing. Not mine. Analog: honest null / the real intended operation.
- `do-the-thing`, `handle-it`, `process`, `manage` — suspiciously generic. Not mine. Analog: name the specific operation and reach for its real verb.
- `foo-bar`, `baz`, `qux` — metasyntactic placeholders. Not verbs. Analog: none; they name nothing.
- `smart-sort!` — invented. Not mine. Analog: `sort` with a comparator.
- `auto-resolve` — invented. Not mine. Analog: honest null — \"resolve\" names nothing standard.
- `do-computation` — invented. Not mine. Analog: the specific computation it was going to perform.

When an invented verb appears, the recovery is to find the real intent. A stray `(frobnicate xs)` almost always meant a real list transform, and *that* runs:

```scheme
;; Whatever 'frobnicate' was for, transforming a list is a real, running thing.
(map (lambda (x) (* x 3)) (list 1 2 3 4))
```

## Verbs from the Kotlin / Swift / C# family (other languages)

Static-OOP languages bring their method-call style. Every method verb below names the same idea as a Motoi function — only the call shape is flipped.

- `xs.map { f }` (Kotlin/Swift) — other-language method call. Not mine. Analog: `(map f xs)`.
- `xs.filter { p }` (Kotlin/Swift) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: `(filter p xs)`.
- `xs.flatMap { f }` (Kotlin/Swift) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `append-map`.
- `xs.compactMap { f }` (Swift) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `filter-map` (discards `#f`).
- `xs.reduce(init) { acc, x -> }` (Kotlin) — other-language, accumulator-first lambda. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `fold` with element-first argument order.
- `xs.sorted(by: <)` (Swift) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: `(sort xs <)`.
- `xs.first(where: p)` (Swift) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `find`.
- `xs.count(where: p)` (Swift/Kotlin) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `count`.
- `xs.all { p }` (Kotlin) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `every`.
- `xs.any { p }` (Kotlin) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `any`.
- `xs.groupBy { key }` (Kotlin) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: fold into a hash table.
- `xs.distinctBy { key }` (Kotlin) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: `delete-duplicates`.
- `xs.Select(f)` (C# LINQ) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: `(map f xs)`.
- `xs.Where(p)` (C# LINQ) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: `(filter p xs)`.
- `xs.Aggregate(init, (acc, x) => ...)` (C#) — other-language, accumulator-first. Not mine. Analog: `fold` with swapped lambda order.
- `xs.FirstOrDefault(p)` (C#) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `find` (returns `#f` on miss).
- `xs.Any(p)` / `xs.All(p)` (C#) — other-language. Not mine. Analogs: `any` / `every`.
- `xs.OrderBy(key)` (C#) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: `sort` with a key comparator.
- `xs.ToDictionary(k, v)` (C#) — other-language. Not mine. Analog: fold into a hash table.

The Motoi verbs that handle all of the above, together:

```scheme
;; map, filter, append-map, filter-map, find, count, every, any — the OOP-analog family.
(let ((xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8)))
  (list (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) xs)
        (filter even? xs)
        (append-map (lambda (x) (list x (- x))) (list 1 2 3))
        (filter-map (lambda (x) (and (odd? x) (* x x))) xs)
        (find (lambda (x) (> x 5)) xs)
        (count even? xs)
        (every positive? xs)
        (any (lambda (x) (> x 7)) xs)))
```

## Verbs from Elixir, Elm, F#, and Standard ML (other functional languages)

The purely functional languages share Motoi's love of composition but have their own spellings. Each entry names the source language so the region is unambiguous — all are region one (other language, real verb, wrong name here).

- `Enum.map(xs, f)` / `Enum.filter(xs, p)` (Elixir) — not mine. Analogs: `map`, `filter`.
- `Enum.reduce(xs, init, f)` (Elixir) — not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `fold` (element-first).
- `Enum.any?(xs, p)` / `Enum.all?(xs, p)` (Elixir) — not mine. Analogs: `any`, `every`.
- `Enum.flat_map(xs, f)` (Elixir) — not mine. Analog: `append-map`.
- `Enum.chunk_every(xs, n)` (Elixir) — not mine. Analog: hand-built chunker with `take`/`drop`.
- `Enum.frequencies(xs)` (Elixir) — not mine. Analog: `hash-table-update!/default` counter.
- `Enum.uniq(xs)` (Elixir) — not mine. Analog: `delete-duplicates`.
- `List.map f xs` (Elm / SML) — not mine as a qualified name. Analog: `(map f xs)`.
- `List.foldl f init xs` (Elm, accumulator-first) — not mine. Analog: `fold` with swapped lambda.
- `List.foldl (x, acc) => ... init xs` (SML, element-first) — not mine as SML. Analog: `fold` directly.
- `List.exists p xs` (SML) — not mine as SML. Analog: `any`.
- `List.all p xs` (SML) — not mine as SML. Analog: `every`.
- `List.map f xs` / `List.filter p xs` / `List.fold f init xs` (F#, accumulator-first) — not mine. Analogs: `map`, `filter`, `fold` (swap lambda).
- `List.collect f xs` (F#) — not mine. Analog: `append-map`.
- `List.groupBy key xs` (F#) — not mine. Analog: fold into hash table.
- `|>` (Elixir / Elm / F#) — pipeline operator. Not mine as syntax. Analog: nested calls.

A probe showing that the Motoi analogs for these all run together:

```scheme
;; The analogs for the functional-language family: fold, any, every, append-map.
(let ((xs (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
  (list (fold + 0 xs)
        (any odd? xs)
        (every positive? xs)
        (append-map (lambda (x) (list x x)) xs)
        (delete-duplicates (list 1 1 2 2 3))))
```

## Verbs from PHP, Bash, and string-first languages

PHP's array functions and Bash string operations represent the world of \"scripting\" Scheme is sometimes asked to replace.

- `array_map(f, xs)` (PHP) — not mine. Analog: `(map f xs)` (argument order matches, unlike `array_filter`).
- `array_filter(xs, f)` (PHP) — not mine (and argument order is list-first). Analog: `(filter f xs)` (predicate first in Motoi).
- `array_reduce(xs, f, init)` (PHP, element arrives second in lambda) — not mine. Analog: SRFI-1 `fold` (element-first).
- `implode(sep, xs)` / `join(xs, sep)` (PHP) — not mine. Analog: `string-join` with list-first.
- `explode(sep, str)` (PHP) — not mine. Analog: `string-split`.
- `count(xs)` (PHP) — not mine as a PHP function. Analog: `length` for a list length; SRFI-1 `count` for predicate-count.
- `in_array(x, xs)` (PHP) — not mine. Analog: `member` (R7RS-small) for `equal?` membership.
- `array_unique(xs)` (PHP) — not mine. Analog: `delete-duplicates`.
- `sort(xs)` (PHP, in-place) — not mine (in-place semantics). Analog: `sort` (returns new list, does not mutate).
- `grep pattern xs` (Bash) — not mine (string-based pattern filter). Analog: `filter` + `string-contains` for literal patterns.
- `sed` / `awk` transformations (Bash) — not mine. Analog: `map` over strings with `string-*` verbs.
- `echo`, `printf` (Bash/C) — not mine for output. Analog: `display` and `newline`.

Motoi side of the PHP/Bash family:

```scheme
;; Motoi analogs for the PHP/Bash verbs: map, filter, string-join, string-split, member.
(list (map (lambda (x) (* x 3)) (list 1 2 3))
      (filter odd? (list 1 2 3 4 5))
      (string-join (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\") \",\")
      (string-split \"x-y-z\" \"-\")
      (member 3 (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
```

## More SRFI-1 verbs that sound foreign but are present

Some SRFI-1 names are unusual enough that programmers sometimes treat them as strays to classify. They are in fact yours.

- `concatenate` — takes a list of lists and splices them. SRFI-1. **Mine.** Not the same as `append`, which takes spread arguments; `concatenate` takes a single list-of-lists.
- `filter-map` — apply a function, discard `#f` results. SRFI-1. **Mine.** Not a stray; check if it sounds like Swift's `compactMap`.
- `unfold` — build a list from a seed. SRFI-1. **Mine.** The dual of `fold`.
- `list-copy` — return a fresh copy of the list's spine. SRFI-1. **Mine.**
- `second` / `third` / `fourth` / `fifth` — positional accessors. SRFI-1. **All mine.** Do not hand-roll with `cadr`, `caddr`, etc. when these exist.
- `last` — the last element (not the last pair). SRFI-1. **Mine.**
- `last-pair` — the last pair. SRFI-1. **Mine.**
- `reduce` — like fold but uses first element as identity on non-empty lists. SRFI-1. **Mine.**
- `remove` — keeps elements that do NOT satisfy the predicate (complement of `filter`). SRFI-1. **Mine.**

Running them together to confirm they are all really present:

```scheme
;; concatenate, filter-map, unfold, list-copy, reduce, remove — all SRFI-1, all mine.
(list (concatenate (list (list 1 2) (list 3 4) (list 5)))
      (filter-map (lambda (x) (and (even? x) (* x 2))) (list 1 2 3 4))
      (unfold (lambda (n) (> n 4)) (lambda (n) n) (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 1)
      (list-copy (list 10 20 30))
      (reduce max 0 (list 4 7 2 9 1))
      (remove odd? (list 1 2 3 4 5 6)))
```

## How to use this catalog

You will not memorize every entry, and you don't need to. What you need is the *sorting reflex*: when a verb fails, ask which of the three regions it came from. If it's module or loading ceremony, the analog is nothing — the library is already here. If it's a fold or hash-table name from a neighboring standard, the analog is the SRFI verb Motoi chose instead. If it's a real verb from another Lisp or another language, the analog is the same idea under a Motoi name. And if it's a pure invention, the analog is to recover the real intent. The catalog above is just those four rules, worked out across the specific names people actually reach for. Every runnable fence in it proves the same quiet point: for every stray worth having, the real Motoi verb was waiting, and it runs.")
